1. Nf3 Nf6 2. d4 g6 3. Nbd2 d5 4. e3 Bg7 5. b4 O-O 6. Be2 ({Bischoff continued} 6. a4 {against Smejkal in 1999 and drew in 17 moves}) 6... Nfd7 7. O-O (7. c4 {was unconvincing in De Santis-LadanyiBudapest 1997. The game continued} e5 8. cxd5 exd4 9. Nxd4 Nb6 10. Bb2 Nxd5 11. a3 Nf4 $1 {and Black was in good shape.}) 7... e5 8. c3 Re8 9. a4 a5 10. b5 c5 11. bxc6 bxc6 12. Rb1 Ba6 13. c4 e4 14. Ne1 Nb6 15. c5 ({In view of what transpires,} 15. Qc2 {may turn out to be a safer option.}) 15... Bxe2 16. Qxe2 Nxa4 $5 {?A free pawn and a big fork threat - but how will Black ever get his knight back to safety?} 17. Rb3 Nd7 18. Ba3 (18. Ra3 {traps the knight but then} Ndxc5 19. dxc5 (19. Qd1 $5 {is tempting but after} Bf8 20. Rxa4 Nxa4 21. Qxa4 {White's pieces are very uncoordinated.}) 19... Nc3 {and if the Queen moves out to g5, then 20...f5 sets up 21...Ne2+ and 22...Nxc1.}) 18... Rb8 19. Qd1 Rb5 $1 {It looks like the knight should drop off with no further ado, but White cannot move the b3 rook anywhere to open up the threat.} 20. Rxb5 $2 {Going down without a fight.} Nc3 21. Qb3 Nxb5 22. Nc2 Qa8 23. Bb2 a4 24. Qb4 (24. Qa2 {was obligatory but offered little long-term hope.}) 24... Rb8 25. Ra1 Nxd4 26. Rxa4 Nxc2 0-1

